## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 128 bit manipulations and zero edge cases without branching.

## Key Concepts
- Branchless bit tricks
- 128 bit layout as hi and lo
- De Bruijn based ctz for 64 and 32 bit values
- Handling zero input gracefully

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline int ctz64(uint64_t x) {
  if (x == 0) return 64;
  static const int tab[64] = { /* 64 entries */ };
  uint64_t y = x & -x;
  return tab[(y * 0x03f79d71b4cb0a89ULL) >> 58];
}

int lsbIndex128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t lo = x.lo, hi = x.hi;
  int a = ctz64(lo);
  int b = 64 + ctz64(hi);
  int m = - (int)(lo != 0);
  int res = (a & m) | (b & ~m);
  return (lo | hi) == 0 ? -1 : res;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if 128 bit intrinsics are unavailable
- Extend to msbIndex128 with branchless patterns

## Why This Is Asked

Tests mastery of a classic bit-twiddling pattern to find the next permutation of a fixed-popcount bitset in constant time, without loops or branches. It also probes edge cases like overflow and zero input.

## Key Concepts

- Snoob algorithm for next higher with same popcount
- 64-bit overflow behavior and edge cases (n == 0 or max with k ones)
- Pure bitwise/shifts approach, no loops

## Code Example

```c
uint64_t nextWithSamePopcount(uint64_t n) {
  uint64_t smallest = n & -n;
  uint64_t ripple = n + smallest;
  uint64_t ones = n ^ ripple;
  return ripple | ((ones >> 2) / smallest);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for arbitrary word size or signed integers?
- What are the performance implications on different architectures?

