## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of branchless, scalable bit-twiddling techniques for 64-bit inputs.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR style bit reversal
- Masking and shifting sequences
- Branchless algorithms for performance

## Code Example
```c
#include <stdint.h>

uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t n) {
  n = ((n >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL) | ((n & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1);
  n = ((n >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL) | ((n & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2);
  n = ((n >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) | ((n & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) << 4);
  n = ((n >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) | ((n & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8);
  n = ((n >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) | ((n & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16);
  n = (n >> 32) | (n << 32);
  return n;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize for smaller word sizes or specialized ISAs?
- How would you adapt for 128-bit inputs and performance considerations?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of deeper bit-manipulation: 3D Morton ordering for spatial indexing, with no loops and tight constants.

## Key Concepts
- Morton (z-order) encoding across 3 dimensions
- bit spreading (part1by2) with masks/shifts
- avoiding branches for tight hot-paths

## Code Example
```javascript
function part1by2(n){
  let x = BigInt(n) & 0x1fffffn;
  x = (x | (x << 32n)) & 0x1f00000000ffffn;
  x = (x | (x << 16n)) & 0x1f0000ff0000ffn;
  x = (x | (x << 8n))  & 0x100f00f00f00f00fn;
  x = (x | (x << 4n))  & 0x10c30c30c30c30c3n;
  x = (x | (x << 2n))  & 0x1249249249249249n;
  return x;
}
function morton3D(x,y,z){
  return part1by2(x) | (part1by2(y) << 1n) | (part1by2(z) << 2n);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to 32-bit inputs? Trade-offs?
- Could you implement a inverse decode that recovers x,y,z from the code?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of cross-limb bit indexing and non-trivial bit-twiddling without branching. It introduces a different angle: packing every other bit across a 128-bit value into a 64-bit result.

## Key Concepts

- **Piece-wise bit extraction with masks:** Isolate target bits using bitwise AND with appropriate masks.
- **In-limb and cross-limb mapping:** Map even bits within each 64-bit limb, then combine results.
- **Parallel bit-pack technique:** Use the classic "compress" trick—mask, shift, and merge repeatedly to collapse bits.
- **Endianness and limb numbering:** The `lo` limb contains bits 0-63, and `hi` contains bits 64-127 (little-endian representation).

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint64_t packEvenBits128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t lo = x.lo;
  lo &= 0x5555555555555555ULL;              // keep even bits
  lo = (lo | (lo >> 1)) & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  lo = (lo | (lo >> 2)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  lo = (lo | (lo >> 4)) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  lo = (lo | (lo >> 8)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  lo = (lo | (lo >> 16)) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL;

  uint64_t hi = x.hi;
  hi &= 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  hi = (hi | (hi >> 1)) & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  hi = (hi | (hi >> 2)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  hi = (hi | (hi >> 4)) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  hi = (hi | (hi >> 8)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  hi = (hi | (hi >> 16)) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL;

  return (hi << 32) | lo;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests familiarity with bit-slicing and fixed-mask patterns on multi-limb integers, not just 64-bit tricks. It also checks understanding of dividing work across 128-bit by treating halves independently. 

## Key Concepts
- Fixed masks for pairwise swaps (even/odd bits)
- 128-bit represented as two 64-bit lanes
- Branchless, constant-time bit permutation

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
uint128 swapAdjacentBits128(uint128 x){
  const uint64_t A = 0x5555555555555555ULL; // even bits
  const uint64_t B = 0xAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAULL; // odd bits
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = ((x.lo & A) << 1) | ((x.lo & B) >> 1);
  r.hi = ((x.hi & A) << 1) | ((x.hi & B) >> 1);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generalize to swap bits in 3-bit groups across 192-bit? 
- What changes if you must preserve performance across SIMD-like widths with additional limbs?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of compact bit-twiddling techniques to count bits without loops, a common interview staple for performance-critical code.

## Key Concepts

- Parallel bit counting (SIMD-style within a register)
- Bit masks and shifts for aggregation
- Portability across unsigned integers and compilers

## Code Example

```c
uint32_t popcount32(uint32_t n) {
  n = n - ((n >> 1) & 0x55555555);
  n = (n & 0x33333333) + ((n >> 2) & 0x33333333);
  n = (n + (n >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  return (n * 0x01010101) >> 24;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to 64-bit?  
- What tests would you add to verify edge cases (all zeros, all ones, random patterns)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of fast, branchless bit tricks and constant-time bit-scanning utilities common in high-performance systems.

## Key Concepts
- Isolating least-significant 1: v = n & -n
- De Bruijn-based indexing: map a 5-bit index to a trailing-zero count
- Handling edge case n == 0 (return 32)
- Static lookup table of 32 entries

## Code Example
```c
uint32_t countTrailingZeros32(uint32_t n){
  static const int debruijn[32] = {0,1,28,2,29,14,24,3,30,22,20,15,25,17,4,8,31,27,13,23,21,19,16,7,26,12,18,9,6,11,5,10};
  if(n == 0) return 32;
  uint32_t v = n & -n;
  return debruijn[(v * 0x077CB531) >> 27];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 64-bit inputs?
- What are alternatives that avoid a static table, e.g., using binary search on bit groups?
